// §5.7 on unpacked array assignment: "Array assignments shall only be done with
// arrays that are compatible. An array, or a slice of such an array, shall be
// assignment compatible with any other such array or slice if all the following
// conditions are satisfied: — The element types of source and target shall be
// equivalent. — Every dimension of the source array shall have the same number
// of elements as the target array."
//
// The clause prints its own verdict against its own example:
//
//     int A[10:1];   // fixed-size array of 10 elements
//     int B[0:9];    // fixed-size array of 10 elements
//     int C[24:1];   // fixed-size array of 24 elements
//     A = B;         // ok. Compatible type and same size
//     A = C;         // type check error: different sizes
//
// which is why both assignments are written here, in the Verilog-AMS spelling
// of `int`. `A = B` must compile — the bounds differ, 10:1 against 0:9, and the
// rule counts ELEMENTS, not indices — and `A = C` must not. A fixture with only
// the failing line would be satisfied by a compiler that refused whole-array
// assignment outright, which is a different answer to a different question.
//
// The expectation was the word `array` rather than a code for as long as no
// VerA diagnostic said it: the compiler reported E0313 "unknown variable: `A`"
// for BOTH lines — the same refusal for the legal assignment and the illegal
// one — and pinning that would have laundered the gap into a pass. E0429 is
// the clause's own verdict and now carries it, so the pin is the code.
//
//! lrm 5.7
//! reject E0429
module ch5_assignment_array_mismatch(p);
  inout p; electrical p;
  integer A[10:1];             // 10 elements
  integer B[0:9];              // 10 elements
  integer C[24:1];             // 24 elements
  analog begin
    A = B;                     // ok: compatible type and same size
    A = C;                     // type check error: different sizes
    I(p) <+ 0.0;
  end
endmodule
